{
  "summary": "Replaces the separate `Warning` and `Error` logging levels and macros with a single `Alert` level and `LogAlert` macro. `LogWarning` and `LogError` are retained as deprecated compatibility aliases for `LogAlert`. Existing warning and error log calls in `src/node/blockstorage.cpp` are converted to `LogAlert` via scripted diff.",
  "problem": "The distinction between `Warning` and `Error` log levels was minor and confusing in practice: `Error` indicated that the node was about to shut down, while `Warning` did not, a distinction the operator already observes from whether the node halted.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'There's little benefit in having two error levels that the node operator should pay attention to when the only difference between them is that one means the node is about to shut down -- the admin can easily see that just by looking at whether the node did shutdown.'",
    "Author references issue #30348 ('RFC: Misused LogError and LogWarning macros') and closed PR #30364 ('Replace LogError and LogWarning with LogAlert')."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#34038"
    ],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Includes a scripted-diff commit converting log calls in `src/node/blockstorage.cpp` and stripping trailing newlines from them.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "This PR combines the separate warning and error logging levels into a single alert level accessed via LogAlert. It addresses ambiguity and misuse around whether an issue warrants LogWarning or LogError when the primary distinction was whether the node was shutting down. The author notes that node operators can already see whether the node halted, making two alert levels redundant. This change is marked draft pending PR 34038, which refactors how log levels are exposed through configuration options.",
